# What should Black play against 1.d4?
teaches: ["openings/black-against-d4"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How does the Queen's Gambit Declined set-up work? What do you do against the London and other quiet systems?"
next: {"title":"Which opening traps catch club players?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/18-which-opening-traps-catch-club-players.cast"}

## Warm-up: hit the pinned piece
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2qkb1r/1pp1pppp/2n2n2/pB6/3P2b1/2N2N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 0 7, moves: [d5]}
      - {fen: r1bqkbnr/1pp2ppp/3p4/4n3/8/2N5/PPPN1PPP/R1BQRBK1 w kq - 0 8, moves: [f4]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: what Black should play against 1.d4. First, two warm-ups from pin day.
    draw position

    pause 0.8

A: Black's knight on c6 can't move: your bishop pins it to the king.
    highlight piece_c6 piece_b5

    pause 0.8

B: Bishop takes c6, check. That wins something.
    highlight piece_b5

    pause 0.8

A: It only swaps a bishop for a knight. A pinned piece can't run: there's more to win here.
    highlight piece_c6

    pause 0.5

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["What is cheaper than a bishop?","A pawn move."] themes ["pin","opening"] voice a teaches ["tactics/pin","openings/black-against-d4"]
        right: Pawn to d5 attacks the pinned knight. It can't step away from its king, and Black can't save it.
        wrong: Which of your cheapest men can attack the knight?

B: A pawn worth one, against a knight worth three.
    highlight piece_d5 piece_c6

    pause 0.8

A: Second one.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Your rook on e1 pins the knight on e5 to the king.
    highlight piece_e1 piece_e5 piece_e8

    pause 0.5

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Hit the pinned knight with something cheap.","A pawn move."] themes ["pin"] voice a teaches ["tactics/pin"]
        right: Pawn to f4 attacks the pinned knight. Black can block the pin, but the knight is still lost.
        wrong: The knight can't move. Which pawn can attack it?

B: The same trick: a pinned piece and a cheap attacker.
    highlight piece_f4 piece_e5

    pause 0.8

A: Both knights were pinned to a king.
    point at.ref piece_e8 gesture circle

A: Today you'll meet a knight pinned only to its queen, and it can still move.
    fade position to 0.35 duration 2.5

## Decline the gambit, then free the bishop
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/2PP4/8/PP2PPPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 2, moves: [e6], flip: true, arrows: [{from: e6, to: d5}]}
      - {fen: r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N5/PP2PPPP/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 4 5, moves: [cxd5, exd5, Nxd5, Nxd5, Bxd8, Bb4+, Qd2, Bxd2+, Kxd2, Kxd8], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/pp1nbppp/2p1pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2NBPN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R b K - 1 8, moves: [dxc4, Bxc4, Nd5, Bxe7, Qxe7, O-O, Nxc3, Rxc3, e5], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bqkb1r/pp3ppp/2n1pn2/2pp4/3P1B2/2PBPN2/PP3PPP/RN1QK2R b KQkq - 2 6, moves: [Qb6], flip: true, arrows: [{from: b6, to: b2}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    box:
      x: 0
      'y': 20
      w: 1000
      h: 660
sources:
    - id: qgd
      title: Queen's Gambit Declined
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 2...e6 frees Black's dark-squared bishop but shuts in the light-squared one; 4...Nbd7 sets up the Elephant Trap; Capablanca's freeing manoeuvre 9...Nd5 10.Bxe7 Qxe7 11.O-O Nxc3 12.Rxc3 e5 in the Orthodox line.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen%27s_Gambit_Declined
    - id: elephant
      title: Elephant Trap
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Bg5 Nbd7 5.cxd5 exd5 6.Nxd5?? Nxd5 7.Bxd8 Bb4+ 8.Qd2 Bxd2+ 9.Kxd2 Kxd8, and Black is a minor piece ahead (for the pawn White took); White thought the f6 knight was pinned.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elephant_Trap
    - id: london
      title: London System
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: The London has become popular among club-level players; ...c5 and ...Qb6, aimed at b2 once the bishop has left c1, is Black's most common response.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_System

    card title "Against 1.d4"

A: Now the opening. We're Black, so the board turns: White played d4, we answered d5, and now pawn to c4.
    draw position

    pause 0.8

A: That's the Queen's Gambit: White offers a pawn to pull ours away from the centre.
    highlight piece_c4

    pause 0.8

B: A free pawn. I'd take it.
    point at.ref piece_c4 gesture tap

    pause 0.8

A: You can: that's the Queen's Gambit Accepted, a real opening. But hanging on to the pawn costs moves, as you saw on day five.
    highlight piece_d5

    pause 0.8

A: We decline instead: pawn to e6, the Queen's Gambit Declined.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

    pause 0.8

A: If White takes on d5 now,
    draw arrow_0

A: the e-pawn takes back, and we keep a pawn in the middle.
    highlight piece_e6 piece_d5

    pause 0.8

    erase arrow_0

A: A few moves on.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Knights out, White's bishop pins our knight to the queen, and we've played knight to d7.
    highlight piece_g5 piece_f6 piece_d8 piece_d7

    pause 0.8

A: White takes on d5,
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: and the e-pawn takes back, as planned.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

    pause 0.8

B: And the knight grabs d5!
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

B: My knight on f6 is pinned, so it's a free pawn.
    highlight piece_f6 piece_g5

    pause 0.8

A: Pinned only to the queen, so it can still move. You've met this trap twice; here it is in your own opening.
    highlight piece_d8

    pause 0.5

    ask "Win material." widget chess line 3 hints ["Is the knight on f6 really stuck?","After White takes your queen, give check."] themes ["opening","trap","pin"] voice a teaches ["openings/black-against-d4","openings/traps","tactics/pin"]
        right: Knight takes d5. White takes your queen, and bishop to b4 gives check.
        wrong: Let the knight take, then count every capture after it, checks included.

A: White's only block is the queen, and you take it.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 2.6

A: The kings take back, and you're a piece up for a pawn. It's called the Elephant Trap.
    animate plies_shown 10 duration 2.6

    pause 1

A: When White plays carefully, the full set-up looks like this:
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: bishop e7, castled, knight d7, pawn c6.
    highlight piece_e7 piece_g8 piece_d7 piece_c6

    pause 0.8

B: Solid. But my bishop on c8 is buried behind the pawn on e6.
    highlight piece_c8

    pause 0.8

A: That's the price of this opening, and a pawn break pays it.
    animate box.x 175 duration 1

A: Which break, once it's safe, frees that bishop?
    highlight piece_c8 piece_e6

    quiz "Which pawn break, once it's safe, frees the bishop on c8?" on_canvas true say_question false buttons_at.x 130 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: …e5: it moves the very pawn that blocks the bishop. …c5 hits d4, but e6 still blocks the way.
        wrong: Which pawn stands on the bishop's diagonal?
        + …e5
        * …c5
        * …g5

A: So the e-pawn goes to e5. But not yet: right now d-pawn takes e5 just wins it. Trade first.
    highlight piece_e6

    animate box.x 0 duration 0.8

    pause 0.8

A: Capablanca, a world champion, showed how. Pawn takes c4,
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: the bishop takes back,
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: knight to d5,
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: a trade of bishops,
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 2.4

A: White castles,
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1.3

A: and a trade of knights.
    animate plies_shown 8 duration 2.4

A: Now pawn to e5: once the knight on d7 moves, the bishop is out.
    animate plies_shown 9 duration 1.3

    pause 1

A: Many club players dodge all this with the London:
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: bishop to f4, and the same quiet moves every game.
    highlight piece_f4

    pause 0.8

B: He isn't attacking anything. I'll just castle and wait.
    highlight piece_e8

    pause 0.8

A: Then he plays the game he knows by heart. Same set-up for us, plus c5, which hits d4.
    highlight piece_c5 piece_d4

A: White's bishop left c1. Which pawn lost its guard?
    animate box.x 175 duration 1

    quiz "White's bishop left c1. Which pawn lost its guard?" on_canvas true say_question false buttons_at.x 130 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: b2: the bishop on c1 guarded it, and now nothing does.
        wrong: What did the bishop protect from c1?
        + b2
        * d4
        * f2

A: The b-pawn, alone now.
    highlight piece_b2

    animate box.x 0 duration 0.8

A: So queen to b6 hits b2,
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: and White should spend a move guarding it.
    draw arrow_0

    pause 0.8

A: Against the other quiet starts, the same plan: d5, knight f6, e6, then c5.
    highlight piece_d5 piece_f6 piece_e6 piece_c5

## Your turn: Black's tricks against 1.d4
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Four for four. Your opponents' d-pawns should worry.
    good: Most of them. The rest will turn up in your games.
    poor: Run the Three Questions from Black's chair, and try again.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1b2rk1/pp1n1ppp/2p1pn2/q2p2B1/1bPP4/2NBP3/PPQN1PPP/R3K2R b KQ - 7 9, moves: [dxc4], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1b1r1k1/ppq2ppp/2pb1n2/4p3/3P4/3BPN2/PPQ2PPP/R1B2RK1 b - - 0 12, moves: [e4], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/2p1pn2/6B1/2PP4/4P3/PP3PPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 0 4, moves: [Qa5+, Nc3, Qxg5], flip: true}
      - {fen: 4r1k1/pp2qppp/8/8/8/2Q5/PP3PPP/4R1K1 b - - 0 25, moves: [Qxe1+, Qxe1, Rxe1#], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [d4, d5, c4, e6, Nc3, Nf6], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    openings:
      - {name: Queen's Gambit Declined, eco: D35, side: black, moves: [d4, d5, c4, e6, Nc3, Nf6], idea: 'e6 holds d5: if White takes there, a pawn takes back.'}
      - {name: 'Queen''s Gambit Declined, Nf3 first', side: black, moves: [d4, d5, c4, e6, Nf3, Nf6], idea: 'The same wall, whichever knight White brings out first.'}
      - {name: London System, side: black, moves: [d4, d5, Bf4, Nf6, e3, c5], idea: 'c5 hits d4, and b2 lost its guard when the bishop left c1.'}
      - {name: Quiet start with Nf3 and e3, side: black, moves: [d4, d5, Nf3, Nf6, e3, e6], idea: 'Against a quiet start, the same set-up, and c5 comes next.'}
sources:
    - id: cambridge
      title: Queen's Gambit Declined, Cambridge Springs Defense
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: After 7.Nd2 Bb4 8.Qc2 O-O, 9.Bd3?? loses to 9...dxc4, and after 10.Bxf6 cxd3 11.Qxd3 Nxf6 Black wins a piece; the opening is named after the Cambridge Springs tournament of 1904.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen%27s_Gambit_Declined,_Cambridge_Springs_Defense
    - id: london
      title: London System
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: ...c5 and ...Qb6 against b2 is Black's most common response to the London.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_System

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you know the set-up: four puzzles from Black's side, so the board stays turned.
    draw position

    pause 0.8

A: The Cambridge Springs, named after a tournament there in 1904: your queen on a5. White's bishop has just gone to d3.
    highlight piece_a5 piece_d3

    pause 0.5

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["A pawn capture that attacks.","What does your queen see once d5 is empty?"] themes ["opening","trap","discovered attack"] voice a teaches ["openings/black-against-d4","openings/traps","tactics/discovered-attack"]
        right: Pawn takes c4. It hits the bishop on d3 and opens the fifth rank, so your queen now hits the bishop on g5 too. White can't keep both bishops.
        wrong: Look along the fifth rank from your queen: what stands in the way?

B: One pawn move, two attacks.
    highlight piece_c4 piece_g5

    pause 0.8

A: Next, a middlegame after the freeing break.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.5

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Push the freeing pawn once more.","Which two pieces would it attack?"] themes ["fork","pawn structure"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","strategy/pawn-structure"]
        right: Pawn to e4 forks the bishop and the knight. If the bishop takes it, your knight takes back: a piece for a pawn.
        wrong: Which pawn move attacks two pieces at once?

B: The freeing pawn, forking.
    highlight piece_d3 piece_f3

    pause 0.8

A: Next: White's bishop went to g5 early.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.5

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first.","A queen check that also sees g5."] themes ["fork","hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Queen to a5, check. The knight blocks on c3, and the queen takes the bishop on g5.
        wrong: Which check also lines your queen up with g5?

B: A check and a loose piece: that's a fork.
    highlight piece_g5

    pause 0.8

A: And one from mating-pattern day.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.5

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true hints ["Look at White's back rank.","Give up your queen with check."] themes ["back rank","mating pattern"] voice a teaches ["safety/back-rank","tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Queen takes e1, check. White's queen takes back, and the rook mates on the back rank.
        wrong: White's king has no escape square. Which capture opens the e-file for your rook?

    pause 0.8

A: Puzzles done. Now the openings these tricks grow from,
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 6 duration 0.4

A: so play Black's moves from memory.
    explore activity openings_drill

B: Against all of them: d5, knight f6, e6.
    highlight piece_d5 piece_f6 piece_e6

    pause 0.8

A: So against 1.d4, one set-up: e6 holds d5. Then c5 hits d4, and e5, once it's safe, frees the bishop.
    highlight piece_e6 piece_c8

    pause 0.8

    erase position

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 470 font_size 110

A: Then find its first mistake.
    text hw2 "1st mistake?" x 500 y 320 font_size 84

## Where next
